  "text": {
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAERETH_ASHLEDGER": "Maereth Ashledger",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAERETH_ASHLEDGER_PEDIA": "The Auditor of Oaths[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Maereth inherited a village muster book in which the same dead men had paid military taxes for three generations. Her investigation exposed a hereditary Order that collected their equipment money while sending their descendants to war without armor. Now a league of free-land towns accepts her judgments before those of the estates, although her insistence that a sworn contract outranks a charitable exception has ruined honest households as well as corrupt ones.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRAN_IRON_MERCY": "Odran Vale",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRAN_IRON_MERCY_PEDIA": "Iron Mercy[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Odran tended prisoners who had survived demonic captivity, and came to believe that freedom itself gave evil its opportunity. His Order of the Closed Hand began by sheltering threatened families, then locked its sanctuary gates and declared that their children must remain until every outside temptation had been destroyed. Its fortress towns now export inquisitors who sentence the innocent for the sins they might one day commit, all while Odran continues to preach deliverance from Hell.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SERA_VELL": "Sera Vell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SERA_VELL_PEDIA": "Mother of the Free Land[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]After three campaigns, Sera returned to find her free-land village serving an estate that would not grant her foreign-born comrades homes. She led the veterans and their families beyond its boundary stones, where they built a fortified town and elected their officers anew each spring. Her growing league still sends soldiers against the demons, but makes the unfashionable demand that victory should earn every survivor a field, a vote, and permission to lay down a sword.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TOVIN_CINDERWRIGHT": "Tovin Cinderwright",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TOVIN_CINDERWRIGHT_PEDIA": "The Breach-Mason[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tovin learned siegecraft by repairing the same border fortress after four different armies broke it. When an Order abandoned the place as cursed, he bought its ruins for the price of its salvage and rebuilt the gate around the tools his employers had left behind. Masons, armorers, and displaced laborers now rally to his chartered strongholds, where any creed may find work but every traveler pays for the walls that keep it safe.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_YSABET_LAST_WITNESS": "Ysabet Renn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_YSABET_LAST_WITNESS_PEDIA": "The Last Witness[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ysabet was trained to recite the names of the ancestors who escaped Hell, until an old soldier asked why her rolls omitted the civilians killed in their descendants' wars. She began staging testimony in the market squares, giving the bereaved the speaking parts and requiring accused officers to answer before their own companies. Her Witness Houses preserve the Bannor's sacred history alongside these less comfortable records, and their militias have sworn that no commander may burn an archive to protect a reputation.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NADIRA_WELLSPEAKER": "Nadira bint Harel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NADIRA_WELLSPEAKER_PEDIA": "The Well-Speaker[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nadira settled a blood feud by tracing two rival wells to the same underground spring and making both clans repair its ancient channels. She carried the resulting water covenant from oasis to oasis, offering every household a measured share in return for labor when the channels failed. Her confederacy honors Lugus, but judges its leaders by whether water reaches the last tent rather than by the brightness of their visions.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_QASIR_OF_NOON": "Qasir al-Nahar",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_QASIR_OF_NOON_PEDIA": "The Unshadowed Judge[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Qasir exposed a caravan master's hidden slave pens by tearing the roofs from his warehouses at noon. Applause convinced the young preacher that every locked door concealed an equal wickedness, and his followers began dismantling shutters, veils, and the screens around family courtyards. The cities under his judgment glow with polished mirrors while informers decide whose private grief must be dragged into the sun, a tyranny he still calls service to Lugus.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYAN_SAFFRONWAKE": "Ilyan Saffronwake",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYAN_SAFFRONWAKE_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Last Caravan[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ilyan began as a spice merchant who found better-paying work returning caravan dead to the families awaiting them across the desert. His embalmers learned the burial customs of a dozen peoples, and their painted waystations became the only places where rival caravans would share a fire. He now governs a chain of prosperous mortuary towns whose contracts promise to bring anyone home, provided someone has paid the fee.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAMET_VEILREADER": "Samet Nahr",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAMET_VEILREADER_PEDIA": "Reader of the Veil[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Samet lost a caravan when a confident sun-priest dismissed his warning that its guide stars were wrong. He spent the next twenty years comparing desert observatories, lunar calendars, and the distorted heavens above old magical ruins. Scholars and night caravans now gather around his cold-lamped towers, where he teaches that reverence for Lugus requires admitting how much the daylight has yet to reveal.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TAREQ_SANDHEARTH": "Tareq Sandhearth",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TAREQ_SANDHEARTH_PEDIA": "Captain of the Guest-Tents[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tareq guarded the guest-tents of an oasis whose elders agreed to surrender a foreign fugitive to a richer neighboring clan. Remembering the strangers who once sheltered Varn, he stood across the tent ropes and persuaded the watch to refuse the order. Refugees and disgraced hosts have since joined his moving camp, which offers sanctuary for one season and demands that every guest help defend the next arrival.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISRENE_STONEEAR": "Isrene Stone-Ear",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISRENE_STONEEAR_PEDIA": "The Patient Listener[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Isrene discovered that the stones of a ruined shrine repeated fragments of conversations spoken before the Age of Ice. Her order spent decades listening, until a nearby village's quarry threatened to silence the oldest voices forever. She moved the villagers by force and founded a ring of protected sanctuaries, preserving irreplaceable knowledge while growing less able to hear the living petitioners at her gate.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DAMAR_NINEBELLS": "Damar of the Nine Bells",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DAMAR_NINEBELLS_PEDIA": "The Protector Without Release[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Damar saved nine villages by erecting warning towers around a sacred valley and drilling every inhabitant in its defense. When the raiders were gone, he declared that the threat would return if anyone were allowed to leave, and placed each village under a monk-governor answerable only to him. His wardens now seize neighboring settlements for their protection, and the nine bells ring to announce both approaching enemies and the capture of escaping families.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELKA_THORNPATIENT": "Velka Thorne",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELKA_THORNPATIENT_PEDIA": "Physician of the Unclean[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Velka was ordered to close a hospice when its wounded began showing the marks of a battlefield curse. She carried its patients outside the shrine boundary and stayed with them, learning which symptoms were contagious and which merely frightened the healthy. Her scattered hospices now shelter altered soldiers and their families under strict precautions, supported by communities that remember the Elohim were healers before they were custodians of holy places.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OLEN_UNWRITTEN": "Olen Marr",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OLEN_UNWRITTEN_PEDIA": "The Unwritten Chronicle[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Olen was an archive attendant until a fire destroyed the only written account of the valley where his grandparents had lived. He began teaching its planting lore, recipes, and names as songs to traveling children, then added the histories that village councils preferred to forget. Communities now follow his wandering schools because their memory belongs to everyone who can sing it, although several great monasteries resent a chronicler they cannot quietly dismiss or censor.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THESRA_QUIETGATE": "Thesra Bel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THESRA_QUIETGATE_PEDIA": "Warden of the Quiet Gate[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Thesra supplied a monastery whose guardians kept promising refuge without counting how many mouths the surrounding farms could feed. During a siege she took control of its warehouses, rationed the grain, and negotiated passage for the children while the abbot still debated the sanctity of the road. Elected warden by the town afterward, she funds sanctuary through tolls and enforceable contracts, refusing both the army that demands free provisions and the supplicant who cannot accept the terms.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREDIEL_THRESHOLD": "Serediel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREDIEL_THRESHOLD_PEDIA": "Guardian of the Threshold[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Serediel once held a passage into Hell open while Basium's warriors pursued a retreating demon host. Mortals fleeing in the other direction forced her to choose between closing the trap and keeping their escape route clear. She stayed until the last child crossed, and now leads angels and mortal auxiliaries who measure a battle by the people brought safely home as well as the demons destroyed.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OTHRA_LEADWINGS": "Othra",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OTHRA_LEADWINGS_PEDIA": "Wings of Lead[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Othra counted the hammers, sandals, and grain sacks abandoned after a Mercurian victory and realized that the next campaign would starve its mortal allies. She established repair yards along the host's route, buying supplies through fixed agreements instead of letting victorious angels requisition whatever they pleased. Her garrison towns are now indispensable to the crusade, but she will withhold a bridge or a wagon train from any commander who refuses to honor the price.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TALVEK_UNFORGIVEN": "Talvek",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TALVEK_UNFORGIVEN_PEDIA": "The Unforgiven[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Talvek returned from Hell with his wings burned and the names of his lost company carved into his armor. He decided that mortal hesitation had prolonged the war, and began binding rescued settlements to compulsory service until the last demon in existence was dead. His followers still fight the Infernals with terrible courage, but execute deserters and take children as guarantees of obedience, making a prison of the liberation they proclaim.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAEVIEL_LASTHYMN": "Naeviel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAEVIEL_LASTHYMN_PEDIA": "Singer of the Last Hymn[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Naeviel welcomed newly arrived angels to the host and heard them struggle to recall the families they had left behind. She gathered their remaining memories into laments, then sent mortal singers to carry those songs back to the villages that still waited for news. Angels who fear becoming nothing but weapons gather around her memorial courts, where a soul is given time to remember its name before anyone offers it a sword.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISTRAN_BORROWEDDAWN": "Istran",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISTRAN_BORROWEDDAWN_PEDIA": "Warden of the Borrowed Dawn[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Istran survived a battle in which allied spells collapsed the shelter he had been ordered to defend. He began building layered wards that smothered hostile magic and insisted that friendly summoners leave their workings outside the same boundary. The mortal enclaves beneath his pale barriers pay for his protection and live under his severe restrictions, while Mercurian captains argue over whether the sanctuaries preserve their cause or obstruct it.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAVRA_WICKHAND": "Mavra Wickhand",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAVRA_WICKHAND_PEDIA": "Keeper of Small Wonders[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Mavra began repairing nursery toys in a refugee quarter where no family could afford a new golem. She fitted broken household constructs with little theaters that replayed the songs and trades of villages lost beneath the ice, carefully distinguishing their stored performances from living minds. Her traveling workshops became the meeting places of scattered clans, and their patrons asked her to arbitrate disputes that the city guilds ignored. She now leads a league of small makers whose first demand is that the poor inherit more of their ancestors than their debts.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRIK_SLUICE": "Odrik Sluice",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRIK_SLUICE_PEDIA": "Master of the Water Clock[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Odrik inherited a canal district whose magnificent ancient pumps had stopped because nobody remembered how to service them. He replaced their ingenious but irreplaceable mechanisms with plain golems that any village mason could mend. Farmers rewarded him with authority over their water schedules, and his maintenance crews gradually became a government crossing several guild jurisdictions. He will sell grain to either side of a war, but closes the sluices of anyone who tries to make his crews into soldiers.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KELNA_BRASSHEAR": "Kelna Brasshear",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KELNA_BRASSHEAR_PEDIA": "The Patient Listener[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Kelna learned to diagnose failing enchantments by listening to the uneven hum inside a golem's chest. After a workshop accident, she discovered that a celebrated master had concealed a spirit-binding inside an otherwise ordinary laborer's frame. She insisted that the bound being be released and that every workshop publish what its animating spells actually contained. Apprentices and cautious householders have since formed a faction around her demand that mastery include responsibility for what answers when a maker gives an order.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VORRA_RENTSHAPER": "Vorra Rentshaper",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VORRA_RENTSHAPER_PEDIA": "Mistress of Borrowed Hands[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Vorra rescued several indebted workshops by buying their golems and leasing the same servants back to them. Each repair introduced another proprietary enchantment, until an entire district could neither harvest nor build without her permission. When its council challenged her fees, she recalled every leased construct on the morning the flood defenses needed reinforcement. The council submitted, and her creditor cities now call submission to her contracts the price of civilization.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DURN_PELL": "Durn Pell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DURN_PELL_PEDIA": "Builder of the Second Door[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Durn was a shelter mason when a hostile dispelling left his settlement's enchanted doors open during a raid. He saved its children through an unfinished service tunnel and spent the following winter designing gates that living hands could close. His fortified workshops still employ golems, but every essential system has an unenchanted second means of operation. Border towns follow him because he promises neither perfect machines nor another retreat beneath the world, only the chance to survive when a marvel fails.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREIA_COIL_OF_LAW": "Sereia Coil-of-Law",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREIA_COIL_OF_LAW_PEDIA": "The Registrar of Peoples[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sereia was trained as a court mage but made her reputation rewriting contracts that assumed every citizen walked on two legs. Her registries gave lamia households, centaur companies, and human guilds enforceable places in the same administration. Those categories also hardened into rules about which peoples could hold which offices, a restriction she defends as the necessary scaffolding of peace. The officials who support her want the confederacy to survive an absent king, even if its citizens must fit more neatly into her ledgers.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TORVEN_LONGSTRIDE": "Torven Longstride",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TORVEN_LONGSTRIDE_PEDIA": "Voice of the Outer Roads[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Torven spent twenty years defending settlements whose brightest children and richest harvests departed for the core cities. When an evacuation order abandoned three allied villages, he held the road until their people could follow. The villages elected him to carry a joint petition, and the centaur companies refused to replace him when the royal clerks dismissed it. He remains loyal to the confederacy, but insists that citizenship include the right to influence decisions made behind distant city walls.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYRA_ROOF_SINGER": "Ilyra Roof-Singer",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYRA_ROOF_SINGER_PEDIA": "Captain of the High Quarter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ilyra organized the harpy messengers who nested above a city that taxed their deliveries but refused to recognize their homes. A landlord's attempt to tear down the high quarter became a week of vanished rent chests and mocking songs delivered through every upper window. Her couriers now protect rooftop markets, smuggle dissidents between districts, and maintain the fastest warning network in the confederacy. She bargains with the city below as its indispensable neighbor, never as its obedient ornament.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AVAR_SENN": "Avar Senn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AVAR_SENN_PEDIA": "The Price of Welcome[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Avar rose by supplying food and housing to newcomers whom the royal administration had welcomed faster than it could accommodate. He converted those emergency advances into hereditary obligations, then purchased offices that let him decide when a family had repaid them. His city admits almost anyone and displays every people of Erebus in its splendid public processions. Behind that hospitality, his magistrates make leaving one's assigned work far harder than entering the gates.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MEREN_GLASSHAND": "Meren Glasshand",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MEREN_GLASSHAND_PEDIA": "Advocate of the Unenchanted Oath[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Meren investigated a neighborhood magistrate who had used a charm to turn public consultations into unanimous acclaim. Exposure earned her a shattered hand and a new following among citizens uneasy about the court's almost religious reverence for its boy-king. Her assemblies require testimony outside magical influence and publish dissent beside the winning decision. She asks whether a people held together by one extraordinary ruler can learn to trust its ordinary disagreements.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAERIL_REEDKEEPER": "Saeril Reedkeeper",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAERIL_REEDKEEPER_PEDIA": "Steward of the Living Granaries[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Saeril kept the seed exchanges between groves whose nobles considered accounting beneath an elf's dignity. When blight destroyed an autumn harvest, his unglamorous reserves fed households that the court's songs had not remembered to count. The groves accepted his common calendar of planting, loans, and repayment, giving their steward influence that neither birth nor magic had earned him. He now trades that influence for secure borders and favorable treaties, regarding a glorious war as a particularly wasteful kind of famine.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NETHRA_CINDERBOUGH": "Nethra Cinderbough",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NETHRA_CINDERBOUGH_PEDIA": "Architect of the Burnt Grove[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nethra returned from border service to find that a magical fire had spared her village's roots and destroyed nearly everything above them. She persuaded its survivors to build around the scarred trunks instead of abandoning them or demanding impossible restoration. Her bridges, cisterns, and sheltered nurseries made the damaged grove habitable while new growth slowly returned. Other burned communities now seek her leadership, and she measures victory by how many people can come home after the soldiers leave.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IOVAIN_STILLROOT": "Iovain Stillroot",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IOVAIN_STILLROOT_PEDIA": "Herald of the Unbroken Summer[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Iovain tended a sacred grove through decades of rebuilding and came to regard every cleared field as another wound in the world's recovery. He began planting boundary trees across neighboring farms, preaching that the forest's return outweighed the claims of short-lived peoples. When hungry refugees cut those saplings for firewood, his followers drove them out and consecrated their abandoned houses as shrines. He offers the Ljosalfar an endless summer purchased by treating everyone beyond its shade as expendable.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AELWEN_MANY_VOWS": "Aelwen Many-Vows",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AELWEN_MANY_VOWS_PEDIA": "Witness of the Short Years[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Aelwen was sent to renew a treaty and discovered that three generations of its human signatories had died while her court delayed an answer. She stayed to record their names and found that their descendants remembered elven silence more clearly than elven promises. Her traveling witnesses now carry the histories of mortal allies between groves, insisting that an oath cannot wait upon the convenience of the long-lived. Border households and younger elves have made her the voice of those who must share a world before they can afford to understand it.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAMAR_OATHMOLT": "Namar Oathmolt",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAMAR_OATHMOLT_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Quiet Boundary[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Namar once strengthened the spells that encouraged a frontier grove to grow, until competing enchantments left its trees flowering and dying in the same week. Undoing the damage taught him to prize limits more than displays of power. His wardens now inspect foreign relics, royal experiments, and druidic blessings with the same suspicion, earning enemies among allies who believe good intentions should exempt them. Groves tired of becoming somebody else's magical answer support his austere promise that their seasons will remain their own.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRUNNA_CHALKVEIN": "Brunna Chalkvein",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRUNNA_CHALKVEIN_PEDIA": "Warden of the Open Shelter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Brunna commanded a mine rescue when a spring thaw flooded workings that three feuding clans each claimed were another's responsibility. She cut through a private vault wall to reach the trapped crews, then submitted herself to the compensation court beside the people she had saved. The settlement established a common emergency fund and gave its administration to her. Her shelter towns admit outsiders during disasters under strict inventories and shared duties, making measured trust a matter of engineering rather than sentiment.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DORRIK_LEDGERGRAVE": "Dorrik Ledgergrave",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DORRIK_LEDGERGRAVE_PEDIA": "Assayer of Lives[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dorrik began as an arbitrator asked to decide how much a collapsed mine owed its dead workers' families. His tables of compensation became a general theory that a person's claim to protection should equal the wealth they preserved for the hold. Rich clans endowed his school, while debtors found that even an honest accident could reduce their descendants' standing. His creditor faction keeps full vaults and orderly streets by making poverty evidence that a dwarf deserves less of both.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HELVI_HANDSEER": "Helvi Handseer",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HELVI_HANDSEER_PEDIA": "Carver of the Unrecorded[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Helvi lost her sight after years reading the ancestral pillars by candlelight and learned the old runewardens' art of knowing a history by touch. While restoring a damaged hall, she found that the names of banished kin had been chiseled away beneath an approved account of their disgrace. She carved those names into a new pillar without pretending their owners had been innocent. Smaller clans now gather behind her demand that the Khazad preserve the whole record, even when remembrance threatens a useful peace between powerful families.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORDAN_OUTLEDGE": "Ordan Outledge",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORDAN_OUTLEDGE_PEDIA": "Captain of the Unclaimed Pass[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ordan's family returned from prospecting to discover that their clan had sold its marginal homes to settle a larger house's debt. He gathered other dispossessed crews into a caravan that opened abandoned passes and lived from salvage, surveys, and armed escort. When neighboring lords imposed tolls, his companies seized the tollhouses and offered the same roads to everyone who paid for their upkeep. He seeks enough land to found a hold, but his followers fear that becoming respectable will mean shutting the gates on people like themselves.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOVNA_TRUEWEIGHT": "Sovna Trueweight",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOVNA_TRUEWEIGHT_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Public Scale[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sovna trained as an assayer in a hold where an illusory shipment had ruined three generations of one family. Instead of forbidding foreign trade, she built testing rooms where dwarf and stranger could watch the same metal weighed, scratched, and checked against enchantment. Her publicly recorded standards attracted merchants excluded by clan monopolies and gave honest small traders a way to challenge richer rivals. The market towns have chosen her to lead them because she treats distrust as a problem to solve before a bargain, rather than a debt to collect afterward.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELISAR_LAST_WITNESS": "Velisar",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELISAR_LAST_WITNESS_PEDIA": "The Last Witness[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Velisar became a shade to remember the names of a village that a victorious king ordered struck from every chronicle. Centuries later, she discovered that she could recall its tax rolls perfectly but no longer remember her sister's laugh. She now leads a company of witnesses who record testimony before fading can empty it of feeling, and brings their records into foreign courts even when Sandalphon's seclusion would be safer.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEMEIA_THREAD_COUNTER": "Nemeia",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEMEIA_THREAD_COUNTER_PEDIA": "The Thread-Counter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nemeia was a mortal factor hired to settle debts whose Sidar creditors had forgotten why they were owed. She learned to translate a shade's century-long patience into leases, warehouses and quiet purchases along the roads beyond the mist. Her trading houses now provision several hidden cities, and the shades accept her leadership because she alone can tell them which obligations still belong to the living.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORVEN_OPEN_DOOR": "Orven",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORVEN_OPEN_DOOR_PEDIA": "Of the Open Door[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Orven kept the ferry to a hidden Sidar city long after its immortal council stopped noticing the villages on the opposite bank. When a flood drove those villagers to his landing, he opened the passage and housed them in districts that had stood empty for generations. The mortal households made him their speaker, and he persuades the Grey that a city can shelter new lives without surrendering the old ones it preserves.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SATHA_STILLGLASS": "Satha",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SATHA_STILLGLASS_PEDIA": "Stillglass[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Satha once painted portraits so that newly faded shades could remember the faces they had surrendered. After raiders burned her gallery, she enclosed her district behind wards that admitted neither fire nor uninvited travelers. Her followers call it the safest place in Erebus, but she now forbids residents to leave and treats every change of loyalty, trade or family as damage to a collection entrusted to her care.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEREV_UNFINISHED": "Terev",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEREV_UNFINISHED_PEDIA": "The Unfinished[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Terev chose fading so that death could never interrupt the mosaic he had spent half his life assembling. He returned from seclusion to find its missing panels displayed as trophies in the halls of three foreign dynasties. His fellow dispossessed artisans furnish a small army that steals their works back, while his refusal to ask who now treasures those works keeps restitution perilously close to conquest.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARIS_TALLOW": "Maris Tallow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARIS_TALLOW_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Lights[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Maris lost her first ship because two rival captains extinguished a harbor beacon to settle a wager. She bought the ruined tower with her salvage share and persuaded neighboring keepers to answer one another's signals regardless of which flag approached. Their pilots and escort captains now follow her compact of safe passage, giving her a coast to govern and a difficult promise to keep when an enemy asks for rescue.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORRICK_SOUNDER": "Orrick",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORRICK_SOUNDER_PEDIA": "The Sounder[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Orrick inherited an Aifon chart that marked a thriving city where his lead line found only open water. He began paying divers and conjurers to measure the currents, ruins and strange voices that other captains dismissed as bad omens. His survey flotilla follows him for shares in each discovery, although he has started recording dreams that predict soundings he has never taken.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SELLA_NINE_SHARES": "Sella",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SELLA_NINE_SHARES_PEDIA": "Nine Shares[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sella won nine shares in a captured vessel by carving its new figurehead from the wreckage of her own boat. Rather than sell them, she divided their profits among shipwrights, cooks and musicians who could make the vessel earn between raids. Her floating markets have grown into a fleet whose crews elect her captain each season, provided the accounts balance and the next voyage promises something worth seeing.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRANNOC_BLACKWAKE": "Brannoc Blackwake",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRANNOC_BLACKWAKE_PEDIA": "The Answered Captain[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Brannoc prayed over a becalmed fleet until something beneath it answered in the voices of the drowned. He returned with full holds and taught desperate captains the offerings that would bring the same favor. They now entrust him with their voyages and their prisoners, while he insists that every sailor carried away by the answering tide has merely been promoted to a deeper service.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYRA_ROPE_END": "Ilyra Rope-End",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILYRA_ROPE_END_PEDIA": "The Unbought[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ilyra learned rigging while chained aboard a prize ship and escaped by dropping its entire mainsail onto the boarding party that had come to sell her again. The freed rowers chose her to command their stolen vessel, and other escaped crews soon joined it. She finances their war on slave galleys by taking the cargo of any captain who trades in people, a rule that has cost her lucrative allies and earned her fiercely loyal harbor towns.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HADRIN_ASH_BAKER": "Hadrin",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HADRIN_ASH_BAKER_PEDIA": "The Ash-Baker[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Hadrin followed adventuring companies with a portable oven and learned that every heroic rescue left hungry people behind it. After a dragon burned a frontier town, he rebuilt its mill before anyone could agree whose statue should stand in the square. Settlers and retired adventurers now follow his workshops along the border, where his authority rests on roofs, bread and the stubborn belief that ordinary work deserves its own songs.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DESSA_VENN": "Dessa Venn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DESSA_VENN_PEDIA": "The Unlicensed Muse[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dessa fled a royal theater after refusing to rewrite a lost battle as a god's deliberate triumph. In the Grigori cities she found audiences willing to laugh at immortals, then alarmed her patrons by giving Cassiel the least flattering part in her next play. Actors, teachers and disputing societies have made her their speaker, and she defends their right to question even the principles that first gave them refuge.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RUVIK_RED_WAGER": "Ruvik",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RUVIK_RED_WAGER_PEDIA": "The Red Wager[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ruvik became famous for killing a priest's summoned champion and refusing the grateful village's offer of a shrine. He taught his growing band that no god could own a mortal, then declared that a mortal should own whatever strength allowed him to keep. Disaffected adventurers and ambitious frontier captains now acclaim him, while the settlements beneath his protection discover that his contempt for divine tribute does not extend to tribute paid to him.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NALLIA_BRONZEBOUND": "Nallia Bronzebound",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NALLIA_BRONZEBOUND_PEDIA": "Warden of the Uncharmed Gate[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nallia was a border magistrate when an enchanted witness condemned three innocent refugees. She spent her inheritance learning how to recognize compulsions and training guards who could resist them. Her wardens now administer sanctuary gates for several towns, admitting pilgrims as people while refusing any priest or mage the power to speak another person's consent.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_EDREN_TWICE_BURIED": "Edren",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_EDREN_TWICE_BURIED_PEDIA": "Twice Buried[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Edren survived two collapsed tombs by sending conjured creatures where a living companion would have died. He returned to the Grigori with treasure, a missing hand and a determination to replace heroic last stands with better preparation. His expedition lodges elect him to direct their ventures, though their increasingly elaborate conjurations raise the question of how much danger can be delegated before courage becomes someone else's burden.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ULSHA_MAREKEEPER": "Ulsha",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ULSHA_MAREKEEPER_PEDIA": "The Marekeeper[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ulsha inherited the ceremonial herd that her clan had kept through the Age of Ice, including mares too old to ride. When a hungry war captain ordered them slaughtered, she led the herd away and shared its young stock with families whose mounts had been requisitioned. Those families follow her grazing circuit and its rites of thanks, forming a confederacy whose riders owe service to the herd's survival before any employer's purse.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HEST_VARROW": "Hest Varrow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HEST_VARROW_PEDIA": "The Unbroken Relay[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Hest first earned a captain's pay by carrying a peace offer through three armies that each wanted it to arrive too late. He built a chain of remount stations whose riders swear to deliver a sealed message without asking who profits from it. The station clans chose him to settle their disputes, and he now defends a growing strip of neutral roads against employers who believe that buying a courier should also buy the news.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CEVRAN_DUSTREADER": "Cevran",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CEVRAN_DUSTREADER_PEDIA": "The Dustreader[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Cevran's clan remembered every mage its ancestors helped Kylorin bring down, but none of the spells those enemies had taught their captives. He pieced those scraps of learning together with riders' weather lore and began shaping harmless dust figures to teach children the old battles. Young riders now gather beneath his painted banners to study a magic that travels with the herd, while traditional captains fear he is rebuilding the very dependence their ancestors escaped.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ERESKA_STONECORRAL": "Ereska Stonecorral",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ERESKA_STONECORRAL_PEDIA": "The Winter Host[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ereska built stone windbreaks around a winter camp after losing half its foals to a storm while the clan's warriors served abroad. Saddlers, smiths and grazing families stayed to enlarge the camp into a town, then built another where the next remount road ended. She commands their militia and supplies their cousins' companies, insisting that a people can keep its horsemanship without requiring every home to fold into a saddlebag.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARRUK_DEBTBRAND": "Marruk",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARRUK_DEBTBRAND_PEDIA": "The Debtbrand[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Marruk discovered that bankrupt cities could still pay a company in pasture rights, hostages and the next season's harvest. He bought the unpaid contracts of other captains and sent their riders to collect, turning one failed campaign into a chain of dependent towns. His creditors' league now offers protection at prices designed never to be repaid, and its riders call every punitive raid the enforcement of an honest bargain.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESRA_VELL": "Nesra Vell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESRA_VELL_PEDIA": "Mistress of Common Sparks[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nesra was dismissed from a prestigious college for teaching its prized warming charm to the washerwomen who scrubbed its floors. She opened a school above a communal bakery, where apprentices learned to mend roofs and purify water before they were permitted to kindle a battle flame. When the guilds tried to close it, the surrounding wards paid her fines in bread, copper, and sworn service. Her following now treats the blood of Kylorin as a public inheritance whose first duty is to make ordinary lives bearable.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OTHRAN_PELL": "Othran Pell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OTHRAN_PELL_PEDIA": "The Unsparked[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The son of two celebrated wizards, Othran could never coax a candle to light and spent his youth maintaining their college's pumps and storerooms. During a siege he kept the firebows supplied after an archmage's quarrel paralysed the official quartermasters, and the surviving captains refused to return his command to them. He governs a league of armourers, clerks, and lesser households that sells dependable equipment to institutions accustomed to solving every problem with another spell. He does not seek to humble magic, but insists that anyone who orders a miracle must first pay the people who make it possible.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAERITH_VOSS": "Maerith Voss",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAERITH_VOSS_PEDIA": "Registrar of the Last Breath[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Maerith inherited an archive whose greatest scholars had died without finishing their catalogues, and she persuaded their families to authorise brief consultations with the departed. Her contracts gradually lengthened the consultations, then made release contingent on completing projects to which new volumes were continually added. The archive now funds an entire district from the discoveries of scholars who cannot resign, while the living compete for grants whose final clauses they dare not read. Maerith sincerely believes that permitting a brilliant mind to rest while questions remain unanswered is the most wasteful form of murder.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ENNAR_SOLM": "Ennar Solm",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ENNAR_SOLM_PEDIA": "Warden of the Quiet Hall[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ennar survived a college demonstration that left a market square repeating the same disastrous minute until its mage exhausted himself. Recruited for his unusual resistance to enchantment, he built a corps that evacuates laboratories, confines failed summons, and arrests professors whose experiments escape their permits. Several towns placed themselves under his protection after he sealed their prestigious academy for refusing an inspection. His Quiet Hall shelters dangerous research only when its owners accept that a soldier with no academic rank may order it stopped.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILVARA_THENN": "Ilvara Thenn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ILVARA_THENN_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Unlicensed Chorus[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ilvara spent years as a military chanter, hiding instructions inside marching songs and stealing enemy passwords from half-remembered dreams. On returning home she discovered that her college had licensed her own childhood songs to a patron who now charged her village to sing them. Her touring chorus began recovering confiscated melodies and embarrassing secrets alike, trading one for the other whenever an official barred its path. Town militias and disaffected chanters follow her because no locked gate or jealously guarded curriculum has yet kept her music out.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HARKA_REEDTOOTH": "Harka Reedtooth",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HARKA_REEDTOOTH_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Last Sled[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Harka lost a foot while bringing a wounded hunting party home, and her chief ordered her left beside the trail on their next migration. The hunters she had saved returned with a sled, then stayed when she challenged the chief and won from her knees. Her pack now judges a leader by how many people survive the journey, giving injured hunters work as trackers, toolmakers, and teachers instead of abandoning them. She meets demands for submission with the same ferocity as any beastman, but her growing winter camps offer fugitives a place where survival need not require another person's death.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VARGAN_SPLIT_TUSK": "Vargan Split-Tusk",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VARGAN_SPLIT_TUSK_PEDIA": "Voice Beneath the Snow[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Vargan first won a hearing by naming hunters who had died in a blizzard and repeating quarrels their families believed buried with them. He claims those ancestors still run beneath the snow and can be called back to judge anyone who breaks a pack oath. Chiefs who accepted his blessings discovered that every unpopular command soon returned in the voices of their own revered dead. His followers feed the ancestral summons with stolen offerings, convinced that a pack which can summon yesterday's strongest warriors has no need to tolerate today's dissenters.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_EDDA_COALHAND": "Edda Coalhand",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_EDDA_COALHAND_PEDIA": "Mistress of the Moving Forge[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Edda once followed raiding parties to collect the broken tools they threw away, learning to weld foreign steel in a charcoal hearth dragged behind her sled. When a captured fortress proved impossible to hold, she dismantled its forge and carried the useful parts north rather than die defending its walls. Smiths and dispossessed hunters joined her moving workshops, paying for protection in repaired blades and choosing leaders by what they could build. She will trade with a city or strip it after a victory, but measures either achievement by the iron her people carry away rather than the songs sung over the dead.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TORVEK_ANTLER_CROWN": "Torvek Antler-Crown",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TORVEK_ANTLER_CROWN_PEDIA": "Caller of the Herd Moot[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Torvek survived three disastrous migrations by persuading rival packs to take different valleys rather than fight over the same dwindling herd. He established a seasonal moot where chiefs stake their hunting claims with antlers and surrender a share of the meat if they break their word. The agreement has made him wealthy in followers and obligations, though he owns no permanent hall and must win the moot's trust again each spring. He accepts raiding beyond the marked hunting grounds, but punishes a chief who starves another Doviello pack as severely as one who flees a duel.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NASK_WHITE_EAR": "Nask White-Ear",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NASK_WHITE_EAR_PEDIA": "The Collarbreaker[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nask escaped an Illian labour column after an overseer's binding spell failed on him, dragging three other captives through a mountain pass. He returned with hunters to break the remaining collars, then kept the freed prisoners under his own command until they had repaid him in service. His raiders now pursue priests and wizards across borders, taking their dependants as guarantees against retaliation. To Nask, Doviello freedom means that no sorcerer may hold his pack by the throat, and the safest way to ensure it is to put every sorcerer's household in his own fist first.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_GENTLE_WITNESS": "Perpentach, the Gentle Witness",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_GENTLE_WITNESS_PEDIA": "The Gentle Witness[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]When a long-silenced healer among Perpentach's inner voices seized the king's attention, he ordered every carnival slave freed and forbade his court to enter an unwilling mind. At the next feast another voice revoked the decree, but three provincial circuses kept the first command and proclaimed the Gentle Witness their rightful sovereign. They shelter the king whenever his gentler facet prevails and dispatch his own signed letters to recruit soldiers against the courts obeying his other selves. The Witness calls those selves usurpers, even as they answer through his own mouth, and his followers fight to preserve a mercy their king may no longer remember promising.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_PERFECT_REHEARSAL": "Perpentach, the Perfect Rehearsal",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_PERFECT_REHEARSAL_PEDIA": "The Perfect Rehearsal[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]After a disastrous royal performance, the voice Perpentach calls the Director issued a season of exact schedules, paid contracts, and decrees that expired at the final curtain. His stagehands and merchants organised a court around those commands, then refused the Laughing Man's subsequent order to burn the accounts for a joke. They claim the Director alone speaks with royal authority and raise armies to bring the king's other courts back to their appointed places. Perpentach himself visits to conduct their rehearsals, denounces them when another facet takes control, and sometimes signs opposite battle orders before the same audience has stopped applauding.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_EMPTY_AUDIENCE": "Perpentach, the Empty Audience",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PERPENTACH_EMPTY_AUDIENCE_PEDIA": "The Empty Audience[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Perpentach discovered that he could briefly quiet an unwanted voice by giving it a summoned puppet and forcing it to perform outside his skull. The personality that savoured that silence founded the Empty Audience, a court of puppets and living courtiers who promise him peace if every troublesome self can be assigned a permanent role. Its ministers obey the king's commands when that facet is ascendant and treat his later pleas to release the captives as interference from the very usurpers they must imprison. Their armies now hunt the Witness's caravans and the Director's stages, while Perpentach can cheer either side because each insists it is rescuing him from the others.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRASSETT_NINE_FINGERS": "Brassett Nine-Fingers",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRASSETT_NINE_FINGERS_PEDIA": "Master of the Hidden Wheels[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Brassett lost a finger repairing a collapsing royal stage, then charged the treasury for the finger as well as the repair. Ministers laughed until his crews refused to raise the next pavilion and the whole court discovered how much of its magic depended on ropes, pulleys, and unpaid carpenters. He now rules a circuit of carnival towns through contracts that guarantee a wage, a hot meal, and a share of the gate receipts. His workers will build almost any spectacle their patrons can afford, but even a king must pay before the hidden wheels begin to turn.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELISSE_UNDERSTUDY": "Velisse the Understudy",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VELISSE_UNDERSTUDY_PEDIA": "The Understudy[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Velisse was a minor demon repeatedly summoned to impersonate absent stars in a royal performance, learning mortal vanity from the applause that greeted each stolen face. During her hundredth curtain call she copied the conjurer's signature onto her own release and walked out of the binding circle in the costume of its tragic queen. Her escaped performers and mimics now enter border towns in the uniforms of their expected defenders, then announce the arrival of an entirely new court. Velisse offers every captive a starring role in her expanding carnival, but keeps their true names and memories as payment so that no one else can ever cast them again.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UGRAVA_CLAYHAND": "Ugrava Clayhand",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UGRAVA_CLAYHAND_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Kilns[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ugrava was the goblin who kept Braduk's communal ovens working when an ambitious war chief melted their iron doors into spearheads. She discovered that the city's unending heat could fire stronger bricks than any travelling merchant would sell her people. Her kiln-workers now accept payment in grain, roof tiles, and promises of protection, binding scattered clans through the things they build together. She tolerates raiders as customers but withholds every replacement furnace brick from chiefs who burn a workshop.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VORGA_COALWIDOW": "Vorga Coalwidow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VORGA_COALWIDOW_PEDIA": "Mother of the Banked Flame[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]After her husband was offered to Bhall for a defeat his chief had caused, Vorga began gathering the old hearth prayers that named fire a shelter against the cold. She teaches that the goddess fell but that her people need not keep falling with her, and refuses the living sacrifices demanded by Braduk's priesthood. Refugee families carry embers from her hearth between settlements, giving her a following no single warlord can surround. Her clan shelters outsiders beside orcs, while its warriors still honour the truces that keep the scattered barbarian tribes from destroying one another.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DREKK_NINETOKENS": "Drekk Nine-Tokens",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DREKK_NINETOKENS_PEDIA": "Broker of the Broken Road[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Drekk survived nine tribal courts by remembering exactly which stolen goods each chief most wanted back. He turned those debts into a road market where caravans can buy safe passage through territory their own soldiers cannot cross. The tokens he sells promise protection only from the clans stamped upon them, so a merchant without the tenth token often pays with a wagon and its passengers. His hired scribes call this commerce, and his growing coalition of toll chiefs makes certain no free road stays free for long.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SARRUK_FARSTEP": "Sarruk Farstep",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SARRUK_FARSTEP_PEDIA": "Finder of Second Fires[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sarruk was a scout assigned to find villages worth burning, but his maps recorded empty valleys as carefully as inhabited ones. When a succession feud consumed his clan's winter stores, he led its surviving families along those unprofitable routes instead of demanding another raid. Now younger clans follow his painted trail markers toward new grazing grounds, carrying seeds and hearth coals alongside their weapons. He bargains with whatever tribes already occupy the land and fights fiercely when either settlers or neighbouring kingdoms mistake that bargaining for submission.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISHKA_CINDERTONGUE": "Ishka Cindertongue",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISHKA_CINDERTONGUE_PEDIA": "Singer of Borrowed Ancestors[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ishka learned that a chieftain could lose an argument to a song his warriors had known since childhood. She began composing ancient victories for new patrons, then sending her pupils to teach the songs in every drinking hall before anyone could dispute them. Clans admitted to her heroic lineage gain allies and marriage partners, while those she names oathbreakers find their burial poles cut down and their witnesses missing. She wants the Ember Clans to share one magnificent history, with every inconvenient ancestor burned out of it.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NERYSS_THORNVEIL": "Neryss Thornveil",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NERYSS_THORNVEIL_PEDIA": "The Unsent Knife[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Neryss spent a century selecting the roads on which the queen's inconvenient relatives would disappear. Ordered to murder an entire winter household for sheltering a Seelie child, she instead scattered the family through her own network of false identities. The agents who helped her now protect isolated woodland settlements, using the same ambushes and misdirection once employed to keep those settlements afraid. She offers neither court the names of her dependants, and insists that a hidden blade can stand between a hunter and a victim.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VAELITH_ROWANFALL": "Vaelith Rowanfall",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VAELITH_ROWANFALL_PEDIA": "Warden of the Sleeping Roots[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Vaelith tended a buried root nursery throughout the last winters of the Age of Ice, feeding the living shoots with carefully cultivated rot. Returning courtiers called his fungus beds a desecration until their own sacred groves began to starve. His woodland communities now move with the forest's cycles, abandoning exhausted settlements before either queen can turn them into permanent garrisons. He keeps the autumn rites and the habits of secrecy, but judges a ruler by whether anything can grow after that ruler has departed.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THARIEL_VOSS": "Thariel Voss",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THARIEL_VOSS_PEDIA": "Custodian of the Second Record[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Thariel once kept the public record of the Unseelie court and a second record of what had actually happened there. During a purge he saved both books, then made the surviving officials purchase the destruction of particular pages with appointments and land. His archivists now require every village to register its oaths, its kinships, and the private names by which its dead may be invoked. He speaks of ending the civil war through an indisputable account of history, provided he alone decides which account survives.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ELYNDRA_ASHGLASS": "Elyndra Ashglass",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ELYNDRA_ASHGLASS_PEDIA": "Lady of the Borrowed Face[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Elyndra designed masked entertainments in which nobles could mock their enemies without admitting they were present. She discovered that an illusion need not fool everyone if each guest believes everyone else has been fooled. Her travelling companies now replace disgraced officials with convincing doubles, collecting taxes and signing orders while the originals remain locked behind the scenery. She imagines a court freed from the accidents of birth, where every person occupies exactly the role she has written for them.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OSSREN_NIGHTFERRY": "Ossren Nightferry",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OSSREN_NIGHTFERRY_PEDIA": "Master of the Unlit Quays[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ossren inherited a ferry whose passengers were forbidden to admit where they had boarded it. He widened the boat, fitted its oars with leather sleeves, and began bringing medicine and foreign tools upriver between the patrols of both elven courts. His night quays have become meeting places for smugglers, artisans and messengers who would never share a public marketplace. He sells passage to almost anyone but will sink a customer who brings a royal census or an assassin's unpaid debt aboard his boats.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEMARA_VEY": "Nemara Vey",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEMARA_VEY_PEDIA": "Lady of the Last Feast[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nemara heard the last words of a household servant whose soul she had meant to consume, and found herself remembering every other voice she had silenced. She swore never to feast again, knowing that blood alone could not purchase the stolen years on which a Calabim vampire depends. Her estate has become a refuge guarded by mortal soldiers and a few immortals willing to spend their remaining strength without replenishing it. She is training a human successor while rival broods gather at the borders, determined that her last inheritance will be a people who can survive her.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRAN_KESSEL": "Odran Kessel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ODRAN_KESSEL_PEDIA": "Chamberlain of the Empty Chair[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]When his vampire master vanished during a border war, Odran continued issuing the estate's orders behind the closed doors of the audience chamber. The neighbours obeyed because the taxes arrived, the watch remained paid, and nobody wished to be the first to discover that the lord was merely sleeping. Odran has used that uncertainty to suspend the household's feeding levies and arm the villages, while keeping the prisons and informers that make the old authority convincing. His coalition of clerks and militia captains rules in the name of an empty chair whose secret might undo everything it has preserved.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VALESCA_DORN": "Valesca Dorn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VALESCA_DORN_PEDIA": "Mistress of the Measured Harvest[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Valesca considered the grand broods' starving villages a disgraceful waste of the people they intended to devour. She repaired wells, ordered clean granaries and established nurseries, then demonstrated that a healthier estate could surrender more victims without emptying its fields. Dispossessed vampires now finance her model towns, where every kindness is entered beside a projected feeding yield. She intends to cover Calabim territory with prosperous settlements whose inhabitants will never be allowed to mistake good husbandry for freedom.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISTREN_VALE": "Istren Vale",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISTREN_VALE_PEDIA": "Prince of the Signed Thirst[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Istren discovered that a foreign merchant would recoil from a slave auction yet willingly sign a contract pledging everything he owned. His counting houses now purchase unpaid obligations abroad and offer debtors comfortable homes inside Calabim borders in exchange for service to a brood. The fine script reserves the right to choose which household member will be surrendered at the final reckoning, and Istren never explains what a vampire takes beyond blood. He dreams of making every neighbouring kingdom dependent on his credit before its rulers notice that their subjects have become his harvest.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RASKA_MORCANT": "Raska Morcant",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RASKA_MORCANT_PEDIA": "Herald of the Open Gift[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Raska was a bloodpet captain denied the Dark Gift after three decades of keeping her master's villages obedient. She stole a brood register and used its concealed family scandals to persuade a junior vampire to sponsor her rebellion. Her followers demand an end to hereditary privilege, promising that any soldier may earn immortality by delivering enough conquered subjects to the new fraternity. Raska has not yet received the Gift herself, but each victory makes it harder for her patron to deny her without also threatening the hungry army she has built.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREV_OF_THE_SEALED_ATLAS": "Serev of the Sealed Atlas",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEREV_OF_THE_SEALED_ATLAS_PEDIA": "The Cartographer Beyond[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Serev entered a planar gate expecting the paradise his tutor had promised and returned with a map of seventeen incompatible skies. He now leads an itinerant college that records what each gate admits, what it consumes, and which promises its inhabitants break. His followers remain dangerous experimenters, but they oppose the final ritual because destroying creation would destroy their only reliable point of reference. Tebryn calls their caution cowardice; Serev calls it the difference between a discovery and a disappearance.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VEYRA_ASH_WITNESS": "Veyra Ash-Witness",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VEYRA_ASH_WITNESS_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Burnt Names[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Veyra was the clerk ordered to certify that a vanished quarter of Galveholm had never contained any residents. Instead she carried its surviving household rolls into the streets, where the names drew relatives, deserters, and frightened apprentices to her side. Their breakaway wards use confiscated ritual diagrams to identify sacrifices and evacuate the intended victims before the gates open. Her faction has not escaped the Sheaim's dangerous magical inheritance, and its claim to goodness rests on accepting that danger themselves instead of charging it to strangers.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PELLAN_OF_THE_DEFERRED_HOUR": "Pellan of the Deferred Hour",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PELLAN_OF_THE_DEFERRED_HOUR_PEDIA": "The Creditor of Tomorrow[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Pellan made his first fortune selling stone to build a sanctuary he knew its patron would burn at the dedication. He now finances gatehouses, pyres, and evacuation barges through contracts payable when the final day arrives. The guilds under his protection preach Armageddon in public while quietly sabotaging any ritual that might make their investments worthless. He wants creation frightened, indebted, and permanently on the brink, a kingdom of recurring emergencies more profitable than either peace or oblivion.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SETHRIEN_UNFINISHED_NAME": "Sethrien Unfinished-Name",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SETHRIEN_UNFINISHED_NAME_PEDIA": "Cantor of the Last Measure[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sethrien once composed funeral hymns for immigrants whose families could not afford a pyre. After hearing a gate answer one of his laments, he founded a congregation that believes every life is a dissonant fragment waiting for creation's final chord. His choirs offer the lonely a place in that supposed masterpiece, then teach them that anyone who refuses to join may be silenced for the harmony of the whole. He seeks neither survival nor reward after Armageddon, only the authority to decide how everyone else's story ends.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESK_DOORLESS": "Nesk Doorless",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESK_DOORLESS_PEDIA": "Warden of the Shuttered Quarter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nesk survived a gatehouse disaster by dismantling the summoning frame from inside its maintenance shaft. The other survivors elected the former mechanic their warden after the appointed magisters fled, and their shuttered quarter became a refuge for workers too useful to be allowed to leave. Nesk trades repair work and captured arcane machinery for food, closing breaches without asking whether the creature on either side deserves salvation. He will shelter fugitives who obey his locks, but he will also seal a populated passage if that is the price of keeping the rest of the district intact.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RHUVEK_TITHEKEEPER": "Rhuvek Tithekeeper",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RHUVEK_TITHEKEEPER_PEDIA": "The Infernal Landlord[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Rhuvek was a collector in Mammon's hell before following the first breach into Erebus, where he discovered that demons destroyed valuable subjects faster than they could replace them. He offers conquered settlements protection from casual slaughter in exchange for perpetual obligations that encompass their dead as well as their living. His officers inventory manes as carefully as other rulers count grain, and enforce the distinction between a tenant and an unclaimed victim. The resulting order is real, but it exists to make exploitation inheritable rather than to end it.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AZHRUL_ASHWARDEN": "Azhrul Ashwarden",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AZHRUL_ASHWARDEN_PEDIA": "The Deserter at the Wall[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Azhrul spent an age rebuilding the same fortress after rival lords of hell demolished it for sport. In Erebus the mason seized an abandoned Infernal outpost and refused the order to reopen its gate to his former masters. His followers are still demons, their cities still depend on manes, and his refusal to hunt new victims does not absolve the crimes that made them what they are. This proposed neutral offshoot seeks a defensible existence outside hell, bargaining for already-arrived manes while accepting the mortal world's suspicion and the limits that dependence imposes.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IVRASHA_CLAUSE_BEARER": "Ivrasha Clause-Bearer",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IVRASHA_CLAUSE_BEARER_PEDIA": "Advocate of the Willing[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ivrasha learned mortal law while serving as the interpreter at a hundred disastrous bargains with the Ashen Veil. She concluded that frightened people would surrender more than armies could seize if offered an enemy to blame and an agreement they could call their own. Her court gives every petitioner a hearing, records every spoken assent, and raises cities from the manes produced by the choices it carefully engineers. She despises Hyborem's blunt coercion because it wastes the exquisite cruelty of persuading a victim that the trap was freedom.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KETHRIL_THOUSAND_SCARS": "Kethril Thousand-Scars",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KETHRIL_THOUSAND_SCARS_PEDIA": "The Pit-Forge Marshal[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Kethril crossed into Erebus as a nameless warrior summoned to die in a magician's duel. Surviving the broken binding, she gathered other discarded fiends and made resistance to mortal sorcery the initiation rite of a new legion. Its soldiers carve the names of defeated summoners into their armor, then demand tribute from any city that once purchased infernal service. She promises her followers that they will never again be tools, a promise enforced by turning everyone outside the legion into one.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OMEL_UNSPENT": "Omel Unspent",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_OMEL_UNSPENT_PEDIA": "Curator of Borrowed Faces[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Omel noticed that a newly arrived mane sometimes remembered a lullaby or a face before torment wore the memory away. He founded a court of reenactors who preserve these fragments in masks, miniature houses, and performances of stolen lives. Descendants invited to his exhibitions often recognize enough to bargain for the rest, surrendering secrets and loyalties to hear one more familiar voice. His city treasures mortal individuality because a particular person can be used in ways that an anonymous soul cannot.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TOVRA_HEARTHMETER": "Tovra Hearthmeter",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TOVRA_HEARTHMETER_PEDIA": "The Keeper of Winter Stores[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tovra kept the storehouses of three northern settlements through a winter their priests had declared a test of obedience. When the rich burned surplus food as an offering, she opened the locked granaries and bound the surviving households into a common ration oath. Her followers honor their ancestors and retain the Illians' religious restrictions, but judge a winter ruler by how many neighbors survive the cold. She accepts snowbound life and opposes Auric's attempt to impose it on peoples who could not endure it.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ELKEN_LONG_SILENCE": "Elken of the Long Silence",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ELKEN_LONG_SILENCE_PEDIA": "The Scholar of Still Things[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Elken discovered an Age of Ice archive whose tablets had survived because their custodians allowed no fire within its walls. He gathered a school that studies memory, suspended change, and the things civilizations destroy in their hurry to become something else. They accept Auric's protection only while he leaves their sealed vaults untouched, and deny that his visions settle questions the records still leave open. Elken will preserve an irreplaceable library before aiding an army, even when the soldiers protecting it find that choice unforgivable.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HROVEN_TITHE_OF_ICE": "Hroven Tithe-of-Ice",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HROVEN_TITHE_OF_ICE_PEDIA": "The Keeper of Borrowed Warmth[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Hroven inherited the only sheltered pass through which his valley could import lamp oil and furnace coal. He extended credit during a famine, then converted the debts into hereditary service when the next winter made repayment impossible. His household encourages the cult of returning winter because every closed road and frozen field increases the value of his stores. He supports Auric's ascendancy while quietly reserving enough fuel to make his own family comfortable in the frozen world he helps create.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_YSREL_THAWLESS_ROAD": "Ysrel of the Thawless Road",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_YSREL_THAWLESS_ROAD_PEDIA": "Marshal of the Winter Caravans[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ysrel learned to guide caravans across frozen rivers after a southern toll lord impounded her family's wagons. Her traveling households now maintain winter routes that bypass the warm kingdoms' customs posts and unite scattered Illian enclaves. They carry medicine, stolen silver, and exiles with equal discretion, taking tolls of their own wherever a weak garrison cannot stop them. She admires Auric's ability to unite her people but has no wish to exchange every distant border for a single master at home.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAEVRA_SNOW_LISTENER": "Naevra Snow-Listener",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NAEVRA_SNOW_LISTENER_PEDIA": "The Avalanche Warden[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Naevra was guarding a mountain shrine when an avalanche buried an invading company and her own relief patrol beneath the same snow. She broke the shrine's ceremonial doors into stretchers and brought survivors from both sides down the mountain. The watch houses that follow her preserve Illian winter customs but keep their shelters open to anyone who surrenders a weapon at the threshold. She refuses Auric's demand to close the passes to unbelievers, arguing that knowing the cold creates a duty to those who do not.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_FIRST_CONTACT": "I speak for [OUR_EMPIRE]. Our decisions are our own.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_GREETINGS": "What have you come to discuss, [CT_NAME]?",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_DECLARE_WAR": "Our dispute will now be settled by force.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_REFUSE_TO_TALK": "There will be no talks between us today.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_ACCEPT": "We accept these terms.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_THANKS": "We acknowledge your gift. Thank you.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_NO_DEAL": "Those terms do not serve our interests.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_PEACE": "We accept peace. Let the fighting end.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_NO_PEACE": "We are not ready to make peace.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_REJECT": "We will not submit to that demand.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_WORST_ENEMY": "Your dealings with our enemies concern us.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_WORST_ENEMY_TRADING": "Your trade with our enemies will not go unnoticed.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_NUKES": "We possess weapons you would be wise to consider.",
    "TXT_KEY_MINOR_DIPLO_UNIT_BRAG": "Do not underestimate the forces at our command."
  }
}
